{"title":"2020 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 15","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solutions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A positive integer divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/6/e56d51636d8b4eee856c5409e18de61ac06b40ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> is chosen at random. The probability that the divisor chosen is a perfect square can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/5/635f84a378488ea1de6ddcbf5081ba779eaad50d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 5\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 12\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 18\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 23$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"376\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/6/e56d51636d8b4eee856c5409e18de61ac06b40ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/8/6c842a03ba520fb9e01f87ebd52ad313b1da3b6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{10} \\cdot 3^5 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"135\" height=\"15\" />. \nThis yields a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/a/b1af856dd06a4208e14bd8f30416e7e0d7b49601.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"106\" height=\"13\" /> divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/4/6d42d092b1949f37b87c923023bfe1e624539cad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12!.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />\nIn order to produce a perfect square divisor, there must be an even exponent for each number in the prime factorization. Note that the divisor can't have any factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> in the prime factorization because there is only one of each in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/4/6d42d092b1949f37b87c923023bfe1e624539cad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12!.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> Thus, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/4/5c412028020034e2bbf482dd09645d6d2ff3b824.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2$\" width=\"53\" height=\"12\" /> perfect squares. (For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, you can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s, etc.)\nThe probability that the divisor chosen is a perfect square is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/4/db4113777e6a63ed02b4da07eadda444458a7ab4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{6\\cdot 3\\cdot 2}{11\\cdot 6\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}=\\frac{1}{22} \\implies \\frac{m}{n}=\\frac{1}{22} \\implies m\\ +\\ n = 1\\ +\\ 22 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E) } 23 }\\]\" width=\"530\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>~mshell214, edited by Rzhpamath\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To prime factorize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/6/e56d51636d8b4eee856c5409e18de61ac06b40ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />, write out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/073bbcb236090c67fa817dd116f730b3ee77c075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2*3*4*5*6*7*9*10*11*12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"275\" height=\"14\" />. Then create a table of primes:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/1/ad10fa5498010d4ce360ac885ada03098c87c484.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(1cm);  int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11}; int cols = primes.length; int bodyRows = 4; real w = 2;   real h = 1;     real top = bodyRows * h;   for (int i = 0; i &lt; cols; ++i)   label(&quot;$&quot; + string(primes[i]) + &quot;$&quot;, ((i + 0.5) * w, top + h / 2));   draw((0, top) -- (cols * w, top));   for (int i = 1; i &lt; cols; ++i)   draw((i * w, 0) -- (i * w, top + h)); [/asy]\" width=\"475\" height=\"238\" />\nThen we can cross out a 2, and add a tally to the 2 column. Then for the 3, add a tally into the 3 column. Then, for 4, add 2 tally to the 2 column. Then continue with this process for all the numbers. In the end, you will end up with\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/4/1e481a366e49bcf1cbfad65bf5f8d0ff0c8067d4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(1cm);  int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11}; int[] counts = {10, 5, 2, 1, 1}; int cols = primes.length; int bodyRows = 4; real w = 2; real h = 1;  real top = bodyRows * h;  real s = 0.25; real th = 0.6;  void tallyGroup(int k, pair p) {   for (int i = 0; i &lt; min(k, 4); ++i)     draw((p.x + i * s, p.y) -- (p.x + i * s, p.y + th));   if (k == 5)     draw((p.x - 0.1, p.y + 0.1) -- (p.x + 3 * s + 0.1, p.y + th - 0.1)); }  void tallies(int n, int c) {   real x0 = c * w + (w - 3 * s) / 2;   real y0 = top - 0.3 - th;   int g = 0;   while (n &gt; 0) {     int k = min(n, 5);     tallyGroup(k, (x0, y0 - g * (th + 0.3)));     n -= k;     ++g;   } }  for (int i = 0; i &lt; cols; ++i)   label(&quot;$&quot; + string(primes[i]) + &quot;$&quot;, ((i + 0.5) * w, top + h / 2));  draw((0, top) -- (cols * w, top));  for (int i = 1; i &lt; cols; ++i)   draw((i * w, 0) -- (i * w, top + h));  for (int i = 0; i &lt; cols; ++i)   tallies(counts[i], i); [/asy]\" width=\"475\" height=\"238\" />\nThen, proceed with the method in Solution 1.\n</p><p>~biegetomcat72\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solutions\">Video Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Education, The Study of Everything\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ipZV6QfN3iU\">https://youtu.be/ipZV6QfN3iU</a>\n</p><p>The Beauty of Math\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ZGwAasE32Y4\">https://youtu.be/ZGwAasE32Y4</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/XVbBKfbvELw\">https://youtu.be/XVbBKfbvELw</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/wopflrvUN2c?t=407\">https://youtu.be/wopflrvUN2c?t=407</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em a